WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.560
AI was supposed to be the biggest&nbsp;
economic story in history.&nbsp;

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.440
A new revolution, trillions in spending,&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.320
“Worldwide AI spending will&nbsp;
total $2.59 trillion in 2026,”&nbsp;

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.240
So, naturally, someone should be&nbsp;
able to prove it made them money.&nbsp;

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.120
Right?
Well… not really.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.560
6,000 senior executives were surveyed&nbsp;
across the US, UK, Germany, and Australia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:32.000
69% said their firms are actively using AI. But&nbsp;
89% reported no effect on labour productivity.&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.000
Another study found, despite&nbsp;
$30–40 billion in spending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.880
95% of AI pilots produced no&nbsp;
measurable impact on profit.&nbsp;

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:47.520
We’ve been using AI for a while now.
So, has anyone actually made money from AI?&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.280
In some areas, it sure seems&nbsp;
to be doing the opposite.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.960
Why is it so difficult to&nbsp;
show that AI made any impact?&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:05.120
Are the end businesses using AI,&nbsp;
actually seeing financial returns?

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:09.680
To be clear: AI has made some&nbsp;
companies a lot of money.&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:16.080
Nvidia. Cloud providers. Model vendors like&nbsp;
Anthropic, consultants, AI API providers, etc.&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.880
What I’m talking about is the end customer.&nbsp;

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:24.400
Those paying for ChatGPT and AI tools, and&nbsp;
integrating them into the end business.&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.720
So, what are the results?
Well…

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:32.800
A report by MIT found that, despite&nbsp;
$30–40 billion in enterprise spending,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.200
95% of Generative AI pilots in&nbsp;
its sample produced no measurable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:45.280
P&amp;L impact, aka profit and loss impact.
95% of deployed AI pilots produced zero return.&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:50.720
Something very interesting is happening.
An enormous amount of AI activity is failing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:55.120
to survive the journey from impressive&nbsp;
demo to an actual financial result…

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:59.040
Another 2026 study by the National&nbsp;
Bureau of Economic Research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.600
which surveyed about 6,000 senior&nbsp;
executives across the UK, US,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.680
Germany, and Australia, found that 69% of&nbsp;
their companies were actively using AI…&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:14.240
But, nine out of 10 executives reported&nbsp;
no impact on employment or productivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:19.680
at their own firm over the prior three years.
These executives also predict, over the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:26.640
three years, AI will boost productivity by an&nbsp;
average of 1.4%, raise output by just 0.8%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:32.800
and cut employment by just 0.7%.
So, not much change.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.400
In McKinsey’s 2026 global AI survey,
37% of respondents said AI contributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:43.040
positively to enterprise-wide EBIT.
Sounds promising, but this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.680
unchanged from the survey from the prior&nbsp;
year, despite deployment increasing.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.960
And the proportion that were AI&nbsp;
high performers, meaning a company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:58.160
who attributes at least 5% of EBIT to their&nbsp;
use of AI… was just 6% of all respondents.&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.640
So, why is AI, this technology that&nbsp;
is seemingly changing the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.560
so at odds with “increasing” revenue.
AI has clearly made some companies money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:13.360
but doesn’t it seem there’s a big missing&nbsp;
piece, that justifies all this crazy spend?

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:17.600
The companies buying AI, the ones&nbsp;
meant to become more productive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.560
more profitable, and more innovative, are&nbsp;
mostly struggling to show it in the numbers.&nbsp;

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.720
Why doesn’t AI show up in revenue?…&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:36.720
And if genuinely useful AI does not reliably&nbsp;
translate into profit… where does the value go?

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:41.120
AI is making a change in some places.
Customer support, for example.&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:46.160
AI help resolved 14% more issues&nbsp;
per hour, on average, and the least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.960
experienced saw gains of up to 35%.
Of course, a “resolved” ticket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:56.800
doesn’t always mean a satisfied customer.
But, this isn’t an actual economic result.&nbsp;

00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:03.520
A support agent handling 14% more issues per hour&nbsp;
does not mean the company made 14% more money.&nbsp;

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:09.440
In other words, there are only very specific&nbsp;
cases where AI sees tangible, economic benefits.

00:04:09.440 --> 00:04:15.520
Klarna claimed its AI assistant handled 80% of&nbsp;
customer-service chats in 2025, and said consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:22.640
satisfaction did not decline, which resulted in an&nbsp;
estimated $58 million in annualised cost savings.&nbsp;

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:27.200
So repeatable, digital, high-volume tasks&nbsp;
and a clear metric, like “resolved”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.720
seem to work well with AI.
This sounds like the answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.520
but there are a few caveats.
Most tasks in work, don’t fall into this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.680
specific case: repeatable, high volume, clear&nbsp;
metric, that perfectly correlates to revenue.&nbsp;

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:47.520
And secondly, and this is the big one, cost&nbsp;
avoidance, is not an increase in revenue.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.320
Klarna may be making more profit from AI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:53.680
but it is not evidence that&nbsp;
AI is generating more revenue.&nbsp;

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:58.720
Most of the AI revolution has been sold&nbsp;
as growth: new products, new markets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.440
and radically higher output—not merely&nbsp;
as more efficient customer-service.&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.560
And, that’s mostly what we’re seeing.
Let’s have a closer look at those surveys again.

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:13.840
From the MIT study, where 95% of&nbsp;
organisations have no return, they found:&nbsp;

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:19.120
“These tools, [ChatGPT and CoPilot],&nbsp;
primarily enhance individual productivity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:24.480
not P&amp;L performance. Most fail due to brittle&nbsp;
workflows, lack of contextual learning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.480
and misalignment with day-to-day operations.”
And I think that last part, is very interesting.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.280
We see this in the McKinsey 2026 survey:
“individual employees are reporting real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.240
impact: Eight in ten respondents say AI has&nbsp;
improved their own productivity. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.160
enterprise-level financial impact&nbsp;
hasn’t followed the same trajectory.”&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:47.360
So we see that AI is great&nbsp;
at improving “efficiency”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:53.360
for an individual… but this is the problem.
That doesn’t directly translate to more money.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:56.640
Think about AI just being plugged&nbsp;
into an organisation, as is.&nbsp;

00:05:56.640 --> 00:06:02.560
AI writes sales emails faster, but does that&nbsp;
improve how many people will buy your product?&nbsp;

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.960
AI writes code faster, and you&nbsp;
might ship more products quicker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.000
but there’s still the bottleneck of QA,&nbsp;
architecture, security, product decisions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:16.240
and the most important one:&nbsp;
customer adoption and demand.&nbsp;

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:20.400
AI summarises meetings… but,&nbsp;
those meetings still happen.&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.840
It helped some employees do tasks faster, but the&nbsp;
company doesn’t actually generate more revenue.&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.040
Cost savings? Maybe, but an increase&nbsp;
in revenue? We’re not seeing that.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:35.360
And, this goes even deeper.
The personal version of AI is easy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:40.640
the enterprise version, is very difficult.
The MIT study also found:&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:45.680
“Generic tools like ChatGPT are widely&nbsp;
used, but custom solutions stall due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.680 --> 00:06:49.600
integration complexity and lack&nbsp;
of fit with existing workflows”&nbsp;

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:55.680
"The hype on LinkedIn says everything has changed,&nbsp;
but in our operations, nothing fundamental has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.880
shifted. We're processing some contracts&nbsp;
faster, but that's all that has changed."

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:05.920
If we look at disruption by industry, note,&nbsp;
“disruption” doesn’t mean revenue here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.280
we see most industries have very little change.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.440
“Only 5% of custom enterprise&nbsp;
AI tools reach production.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:18.800
Chatbots succeed because they're easy to try and&nbsp;
flexible, but fail in critical workflows due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.720
lack of memory and customization.”
So it seems part of the mystery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:30.400
is beyond “deployment”, or “just using more AI”,&nbsp;
but the entire company and workflow around the AI.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:35.120
“High performers pursue growth and/or&nbsp;
innovation alongside efficiency; they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:41.120
fundamentally redesign workflows that are enabled&nbsp;
by AI rather than insert AI into existing ones”

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:47.200
ChatGPT can draft an answer to a customer&nbsp;
question… but, to solve the customer’s problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:52.080
it might need to be able to navigate their&nbsp;
account, check the order, understand the contract,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:58.240
have authority to issue refunds, interact with the&nbsp;
CRM, access data across multiple dashboards. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:03.440
it’s not allowed to do any of those things.
And for good reason.&nbsp;

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:07.920
With those permissions, mistakes&nbsp;
can be disastrous and expensive.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:13.280
But, we’re at a weird paradox here.
The AI can write the refund email. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:16.960
just cannot be trusted to refund the&nbsp;
right person, for the right amount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.240
under the right policy, without&nbsp;
staff writing and building lots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.560
of infrastructure and information for it.
Enterprise tools stall because of brittle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.960
workflows. Maybe not brittle for&nbsp;
humans, but brittle for AI models.

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:34.240
Another PwC survey found that most companies&nbsp;
were lacking the “AI foundations, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:38.720
clearly defined road maps and sufficient&nbsp;
levels of investment” to realise a return.&nbsp;

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:44.000
To really get the most out of AI, you sort&nbsp;
of have to build lots of the business for AI.&nbsp;

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.640
We’ve actually seen something&nbsp;
very similar to this before.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:51.360
In Stanford economist Paul David’s 1990&nbsp;
paper “The Dynamo and the Computer”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:56.640
he found that managers typically overlaid “one&nbsp;
technical system upon a preexisting stratum”.&nbsp;

00:08:56.640 --> 00:09:01.200
In the late 19th century, when factories that&nbsp;
originally used a waterwheel or steam engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:03.920
first introduced electricity,&nbsp;
managers simply replaced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:09.120
old power sources with new electric motors.
“They enjoyed modest cost savings on fuel as well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:14.640
as slightly improved control, but their factories&nbsp;
continued to function exactly as before.”&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:18.960
This is the Dynamo Fallacy, and&nbsp;
doesn’t this sound familiar?&nbsp;

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:23.520
“Electrification changed manufacturing,&nbsp;
but only after managers became willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:28.560
to redesign their entire businesses around the&nbsp;
fundamental capabilities of electric machinery.”&nbsp;

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:30.720
Leaders falsely assume a groundbreaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.640
new technology, will immediately&nbsp;
deliver massive productivity gains.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:37.600
Maybe, AI is struggling, as&nbsp;
people are just putting another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:40.720
“technical system upon a preexisting stratum”.&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.560
Maybe it is because most companies are using&nbsp;
AI in the easiest possible way: as a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:54.080
personal tool, inserted into an old organisation?
But, how do you rebuild an organisation around AI?&nbsp;

00:09:54.080 --> 00:10:05.520
And even if you do, does that actually&nbsp;
promise an increase in revenue?

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:09.600
If you’ve watched this far, you&nbsp;
probably like our videos! So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.240
consider subscribing! It helps the channel&nbsp;
and means you won’t miss future videos.

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.320
It’s hard to say what this looks&nbsp;
like, or what this looks like for AI.&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:22.320
But, there are a few explanations&nbsp;
on why this hasn’t happened yet.&nbsp;

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.720
One is something called the J-curve.

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:29.520
It’s a theory that explains how productivity&nbsp;
can fall or look weak early in rollout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:35.200
because it takes reorganisation, new processes,&nbsp;
and new training before benefits become visible.&nbsp;

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:40.240
Like a J, it goes down first, then up.
A bunch of people say this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:44.720
why AI results are disappointing.
But, we’re not quite seeing signs of the curve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:50.160
turning upward. It just explains delayed results,&nbsp;
not how you actually deliver those results.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:55.760
Next, is a more plausible theory I think: David&nbsp;
J. Teece’s theory of Complementary Assets.&nbsp;

00:10:55.760 --> 00:11:00.880
Profits from innovation often go not to the&nbsp;
inventor or user of the technology, but to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:07.040
whoever controls the scarce complementary assets.
“If the complements are not owned by, or cannot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:11.520
easily be replicated by, the inventor, then&nbsp;
rents will likely be drained away toward the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:16.960
complement owner(s), and the innovator will&nbsp;
be in no better position than an imitator who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:21.440
controls relevant complements.”
I think this is fascinating.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:25.840
There are two parts to this, value&nbsp;
creation, and value capture. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.840 --> 00:11:30.400
don’t always happen at the same place.
Even if AI makes you more efficient,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:34.720
the money goes to whoever owns the “scarce&nbsp;
complement”. The thing that is needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:39.600
turn a technology into a valuable product or&nbsp;
service that is hard to replace or obtain.&nbsp;

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:44.320
Distribution, maybe the integrated&nbsp;
tool providers, but most likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:50.080
data centre owners, and GPU capacity.
Companies buying AI may save a little time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.080 --> 00:11:53.840
while the companies controlling chips,&nbsp;
cloud capacity, enterprise distribution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:57.920
or the customer relationship&nbsp;
capture most of the economic upside.&nbsp;

00:11:57.920 --> 00:12:03.040
The companies convincing everyone that&nbsp;
AI will bring about a massive return.

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:08.720
So, has AI made anyone money? Obviously.
But it’s the companies selling chips,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:14.640
cloud capacity, models, and AI services,&nbsp;
not the average business deploying AI.&nbsp;

00:12:14.640 --> 00:12:18.240
The ones who control the assets,&nbsp;
are the ones who seem to capture it.&nbsp;

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:21.840
But, AI can be useful without&nbsp;
translating to more money.&nbsp;

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:25.280
Because, it’s one thing to make&nbsp;
your whole company more efficient.&nbsp;

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:26.720
But, to actually capture that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:32.080
financially? Or to prove that efficiency&nbsp;
made you more money? That’s not as clear.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:35.360
Something else very interesting&nbsp;
is happening with AI.&nbsp;

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:39.840
Namely, the most important part of&nbsp;
the industry, is actually moving.&nbsp;

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:41.840
Moving away from the models.&nbsp;

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:47.040
This is a reason OpenAI is in a lot of trouble.
They’ve promised about a trillion in spend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.760
but ironically, they’re caught in the layer&nbsp;
of the stack that might be the least valuable.&nbsp;

00:12:51.760 --> 00:13:04.240
AI models, might be slowly becoming a commodity.
